WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.202
-Welcome back to "Late Night".

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:03.470
We are here with
Senator Bernie Sanders.

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:04.871
You have always been
incredibly consistent

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:08.041
with your political views,
and it was resurfaced

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:10.577
this year around New Year's
that, in 1978,

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:12.511
your local newspaper
was asking people for their

00:00:12.511 --> 00:00:14.880
New Year's resolutions,
and, in 1978 --

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.316
We can take
a look at it here.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:21.321 line:0%
You were still very focused on
things like wage inequality

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:24.990 line:0%
and, you know, people on the
bottom of the economic ladder

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.325 line:0%
getting their fair shake.

00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:30.496
So this has been something that
has been true of you

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:34.400
through every, I would imagine,
New Year's resolution cycle.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.735
-Well, Seth, you know,
I came from a family

00:00:36.735 --> 00:00:38.337
that did not have
a lot of money.

00:00:38.337 --> 00:00:40.106
We lived in
a rent-controlled apartment.

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:44.877
And I try very hard
not to forget where I came from.

00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:47.880
-You recently gave an
interview with "The Guardian"

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.152
where you echoed
what you believed in 1978

00:00:53.152 --> 00:00:55.488
about the need for
the Democratic Party

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.724
to reconnect with
working-class voters.

00:00:57.724 --> 00:01:00.360
Why is it, do you feel,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.861
that it has been so hard to
make that connection

00:01:02.861 --> 00:01:05.164
stronger in recent years?

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:07.901
-Well, look,
here is the reality,

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:11.304
and it's a reality that just is
not talked about very much

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:13.906
in the corporate media
or talked about, you know,

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:17.677
in the political process,

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:21.447
and that is, we are
moving toward an oligarchy.

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:24.250
Now, do you hear many people
talking about that, Seth?

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.620
-Every now and then,
I try to bring it up.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.456
-But, you know, it just --
And that means you're looking at

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:33.192
a very small number
of billionaires,

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:35.962
a few hundred billionaires,
who exercise

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:38.398
unbelievable control over
the economic and political life

00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:39.666
of this country,

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:42.335
and these guys are becoming
phenomenally richer.

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:44.570
The rich today in America
are better off than

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.273
the rich have ever been
in the history of this country.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:48.841
That's one reality.

00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:50.209
And the other reality is,

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:52.344
you got half of our people
living paycheck-to-paycheck.

00:01:52.344 --> 00:01:54.747
People are going to work today,

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:58.517
putting their lives on the line,
because they, you know,

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:01.320
may get exposed to COVID,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.557
because they got to
feed their kids.

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:07.994
We have 90 million people
who have

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:10.930
no health insurance or
are underinsured.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.032
You got families
that can't afford childcare.

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:15.701
You got all of the workers
who have nothing in the bank

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:18.738
as they face retirement.

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:21.841
That is the economic
reality facing America.

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.542
And because of the power of

00:02:23.542 --> 00:02:27.279
money and
campaign contributions,

00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:32.051
those needs of working people
have been long neglected.

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:33.552
Now, I think we are making,

00:02:33.552 --> 00:02:36.289
under President Biden and under
the Democratic Congress,

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:39.392
some steps forward,
some progress on that.

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:41.661
The American --
The rescue plan was

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:43.997
extraordinarily important
in protecting

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:45.198
the needs of working people.

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:46.665
The infrastructure bill
we passed --

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:50.703
gonna create hundreds of
thousands of good-paying jobs.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:53.072
But at the end of the day,
the Democratic Party --

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:54.607
You know, the Republican Party

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:56.876
has become a
right-wing extremist party.

00:02:56.876 --> 00:03:00.312
It represents the wealthy.
It represents the powerful.

00:03:00.312 --> 00:03:01.781
Fine. I accept that.

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:04.784
But what the Democratic Party
has got to do is

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:06.819
to make it clear,
which side are they on?

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:09.755
Do you remember that old
union folk song,

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:11.691
"Which Side Are You On?"

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:14.260
-That was -- We played it
a lot in high school.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.696
[ Both laugh ]

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:18.497
-Well, we should
continue to play it,

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:21.300
because that is the very simple
and most profound

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.204
political question there is --
which side are you on?

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:25.504
You know,
we can disagree about nuance.

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:30.108
Are you prepared to stand
with struggling working families

00:03:30.108 --> 00:03:31.944
all over this country?

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:35.113
And are you prepared to take on
Big Money interests?

00:03:35.113 --> 00:03:36.248
That is the question.

00:03:36.248 --> 00:03:40.219
-We have seen, you know,
some organized labor win

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:42.455
some victories in recent months.

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:43.823
John Deere.

00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:47.927
You were recently in Michigan
for a Kellogg's strike.

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:51.230
Some nurses
have won some victories.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:55.101
Do you see this as a positive
move in the right direction

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:57.837
or just very small victories?

00:03:57.837 --> 00:03:59.771
-Absolutely. No, I think
what you're seeing --

00:03:59.771 --> 00:04:03.309
And young people at Starbucks
in Buffalo, New York,

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:09.015
became the first Starbucks
coffee shop to be unionized.

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:11.450
You are seeing
all over this country,

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:15.054
with incredible courage,
working people

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.489
standing up
and saying to their

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:21.126
very powerful and wealthy
owners, "You know what?

00:04:21.126 --> 00:04:23.062
You're not going to take away
our healthcare.

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:25.898
You know what?
We deserve decent wages.

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:28.667
We deserve decent pensions."

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:32.471
And you're seeing working people
all over this country

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:34.673
standing up and fighting back.

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:40.679
And to me, that is just
extraordinarily impressive,

00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:43.249
inspirational,
and I think it's something

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:45.551
that we have all got to support.

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:48.187
We're involved right now
in three strikes in

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.790
West Virginia
and Alabama and California.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:55.093
We have brave, incredibly brave,
people out on strike right now

00:04:55.093 --> 00:04:59.866
without any healthcare,
without any wages coming in,

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:02.067
taking on powerful
special interests.

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:08.440
So I think that is an indication
of the frustration

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.877
that many workers are feeling
around this country

00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:13.445
and their preparedness to
stand up and fight back,

00:05:13.445 --> 00:05:14.780
and that's a great thing.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:16.482
-When you are at
these strikes,

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:17.850
particularly when
they are in red strate--

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.085
red states -- excuse me --
like Alabama, you mentioned,

00:05:20.085 --> 00:05:23.322
are you cognizant of
or do you feel it's true

00:05:23.322 --> 00:05:26.025
that maybe a lot of the people
that you are there fighting for

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:28.694
are not Bernie Sanders
supporters

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:31.463
or even Democratic voters?

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:33.633
-Well, that's
an interesting question.

00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:36.802
I happen to believe that
with the --

00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:40.773
If you make it clear to
working people,

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:44.310
whether you're in Alabama,
California, or anyplace else,

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:47.547
that you are prepared to
stand up and fight

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:49.815
for their interests,
that you have the guts

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:52.084
to tell the
pharmaceutical industry,

00:05:52.084 --> 00:05:53.886
"You're not going to
charge us 10 times more

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.556
for a drug that
they do in Canada,"

00:05:56.556 --> 00:05:58.858
that you're going to fight
for healthcare for all

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:01.861
as a human right,
that you're going to create jobs

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:06.598
which pay at least $15 an hour,
minimum wage that we need.

00:06:06.598 --> 00:06:10.403
See, I happen to believe that
the Republican success

00:06:10.403 --> 00:06:13.072
in these red states among
working people

00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:16.576
is not anything that
they have done, per se.

00:06:16.576 --> 00:06:20.246
It's not that, in red states,
people believe in tax breaks

00:06:20.246 --> 00:06:23.048
for billionaires
or throwing millions of people

00:06:23.048 --> 00:06:25.318
off of health insurance
or that people

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:28.320
in red states want to do,
as Mitch McConnell does,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.123
cut social security, Medicare,
and Medicaid.

00:06:31.123 --> 00:06:33.726
I think the reason
those states have gone red

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:36.128
is that people are looking at
the Democratic Party and saying,

00:06:36.128 --> 00:06:37.129
"We don't believe you.

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:38.197
We don't trust you.

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:40.032
You're really not
fighting for us."

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:44.470
And then the reasons why they
go toward the Republicans.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:47.873
So when I go to those states
and I say, "You know what?

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:51.444
You're entitled to decent wages
and decent benefits,

00:06:51.444 --> 00:06:52.912
that healthcare is
a human right,

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:55.848
that your kids have
a right to go to college,

00:06:55.848 --> 00:06:56.916
that you shouldn't be ripped off

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:59.018
by the pharmaceutical
industry,"

00:06:59.018 --> 00:07:00.352
people nod their heads
and they say,

00:07:00.352 --> 00:07:01.554
"Yeah, you're right, Bernie."

00:07:01.554 --> 00:07:04.056
No different in
those states than in any --

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:05.991
You know, Vermont
or any other state.

00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:08.227
-Well, thank you, as always,
for making time for us.

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:10.563
When you're out on those picket
lines, make sure you bundle up.

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:12.097
I know you're very good at it,
but I do --

00:07:12.097 --> 00:07:14.300
You know, I'm still concerned.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.069
-Okay. Seth, thanks very much.
Keep up the great work.

00:07:17.069 --> 00:07:18.871
-Alright. Thank you so much
for being with us, Senator.

